        "reasoning_text": "Now let me analyze the domain brief and identify all subdomains. Let me think through this carefully.\n\nThe domain brief describes a **romantic fantasy fiction writing system** with these major areas:\n\n## Pipeline Phases (the core creative workflow)\n1. **Concept Development** - Idea distillation, theme extraction, premise refinement, comp titles\n2. **Worldbuilding** - Geography, magic system, politics, history, culture, fauna/flora, consistency bible\n3. **Character Development** - Protagonist profiles, romance arc design, supporting cast, antagonist, voice guides, relationship web\n4. **Plotting & Outlining** - Story structure selection, chapter-level outlines, tension mapping, subplot tracking, dual-arc interleave\n\n\n5. **Prose Style Development** - Voice calibration, style guide, tone palette\n6. **Drafting** - Chapter drafting, continuity tracking, draft segmentation\n7. **Self-Editing & Revision** - Developmental edit, line edit, copy edit (multi-pass)\n8. **Beta Reading Simulation** - Romance reader, fantasy reader, craft reader, sensitivity reader, originality reader lenses\n9. **Polish & Delivery** - Final proofread, chapter summaries, series KB update, delivery packaging\n\nThe system also includes cross-cutting concerns like adversarial consistency gates that audit each phase and series architecture considerations.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the advanced craft dimensions that weave through the entire process: foreshadowing and symbolism work with ledgers and motif tracking, multi-POV engineering that handles voice calibration and information asymmetry, emotional resonance through throughlines and sensory anchoring, reader experience design focused on hooks and pacing, and dialogue systems that layer function and subtext.\n\nI'm organizing the system architecture around a user-facing guide agent as the entry point that collects story ideas, style samples, mood direction, character sketches, world fragments, and constraints, then validates everything and writes the configuration before launching the pipeline. From there I'm breaking this into distinct subdomains: the input handling and guide agent, concept development as phase one covering idea distillation and theme extraction, and I'm starting to map out how these feed into the broader craft systems. since it happens during concept).\n\n**SD-003: worldbuilding** — Phase 2. Geography, magic system design (Sanderson's Laws), political/social structures, history/lore, culture/daily life, fauna/flora, consistency bible. This is high complexity due to the number of subcategories.\n\n**SD-004: character-development** — Phase 3. Protagonist profiles, romance arc design, supporting cast, antagonist design, character voice guides, relationship web.\n\n**SD-005: plotting-and-outlining** — Phase 4. Story structure selection (multiple frameworks), chapter-level outline, tension mapping, subplot tracking, dual-arc interleave (fantasy + romance). This includes the foreshadowing ledger and reader experience design elements that are architected during plotting.\n\n**SD-006: prose-style-calibration** — Phase 5. Voice calibration from reference material, style guide creation, tone palette definition, and multi-POV voice calibration matrix.\n\n**SD-007: chapter-drafting** — Phase 6. Chapter-by-chapter prose generation following outlines, maintaining consistency, continuity tracking, chapter hooks and page-turners.\n\n**SD-008: revision-and-editing** — Phase 7. Multi-pass revision: developmental edit, line edit, copy edit. Each with specific focus areas.\n\n**SD-009: beta-reading-simulation** — Phase 8. Five independent reader lenses: romance, fantasy, craft, sensitivity, originality. Each produces structured findings.\n\n**SD-010: polish-and-delivery** — Phase 9. Final proofread, chapter summaries, delivery packaging.\n\n**SD-011: adversarial-consistency-gates** — Cross-cutting. Phase-specific auditors that challenge each phase's output with a kill-your-darlings mandate.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the cross-cutting subdomains that weave through the entire creative process. Series knowledge management handles the artifact organization and sequel contracts, continuity tracking maintains the running facts and character knowledge matrices across the whole book, and advanced craft integration covers the foreshadowing, symbolism, dialogue, and thematic architecture that spans multiple phases. Though I'm wondering if some of these craft dimensions should actually be folded into the phases where they're most active rather than kept separate. them as a single subdomain that produces shared skills/knowledge artifacts.\n\nActually, I need to step back and think about the actual agent mapping. The brief lays out nine pipeline phases, each with an adversarial auditor pattern, plus series architecture and advanced craft dimensions as cross-cutting concerns. Let me map this to actual subdomains: user guide and input handling, concept development with craft profile selection, worldbuilding with its many subcategories, character development with multiple aspects, and plotting with its complex frameworks—\n\nthen prose calibration, chapter drafting with continuity work, revision with distinct pass types, beta reading simulation across five lenses, and final polish. Beyond the core phases, I need agents for the adversarial consistency checks across creative work, series knowledge management for artifact organization and sequel support, continuity tracking with running trackers and promise matrices, and craft knowledge systems covering foreshadowing, symbolism, emotional resonance, and dialogue.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the specific agent count needed for each major function. The user guide is minimal, but concept work needs a specialist plus a craft profile selector. Worldbuilding scales to three or four agents depending on whether I use a coordinator or separate geography, magic, and politics specialists. Character work needs a developer and romance arc designer plus voice guidance. Plotting requires structure selection, outline generation, tension mapping, and dual-arc interleaving. Prose style can be handled by one or two agents, chapter drafting needs a writer, continuity tracker, and hook engineer, and revision work scales to three or four agents for developmental passes.\n\nFor beta-reading, I'm thinking five or six agents—one per lens plus a synthesis coordinator. Polish and delivery is lean at one or two agents. I'm also building in adversarial consistency checks that repeat across phases, series knowledge management, continuity tracking with a knowledge matrix, and craft knowledge specialists for foreshadowing, emotional resonance, and dialogue. This brings me to roughly thirty to forty-two agents total, which fits comfortably within the constraints. I'm reconsidering the continuity layer now to see if there's overlap I can consolidate.\n\nActually, continuity tracking shows up in multiple places—the drafting phase maintains the tracker, beta readers audit it, and it's part of the advanced craft dimensions. But since it has its own artifact directory with dedicated files and gets referenced across phases, I think it warrants its own subdomain rather than folding it into drafting. Let me lock in the final structure starting with user guide and input, then concept development.\n\nContinuing through the subdomains: concept development handles idea distillation and craft profile selection with two agents, worldbuilding covers the full scope of geography through fauna with three agents, character development tackles protagonist through relationship mapping with three agents, plotting and outlining manages structure through foreshadowing with four agents, prose style calibration handles reference analysis and voice work with two agents, and chapter drafting is high complexity.\n\nThen revision and editing runs four agents through developmental to copy edit passes, beta reading simulation uses six agents across five lenses plus synthesis, polish and delivery handles final proofread and packaging, adversarial consistency enforces quality through per-phase auditors, series knowledge management maintains the KB and cross-book consistency, and continuity tracking rounds out the system.\n\nI'm looking at the full agent count now — roughly 39 agents total across all these systems, which feels manageable. Time to start building out the actual artifacts.",
