        "reasoning_text": "Now I have all the information I need. Let me systematically extract every invariant from:\n\n1. **invariants.md Part 1 (Explicit Invariants)** - Lines 14-138, confidence 0.9-1.0\n2. **invariants.md Part 2 (Craft Toolbox)** - Lines 141-231, confidence 0.9-1.0 (explicitly stated as binding when selected)\n3. **domain-brief.md (Implicit Invariants)** - Lines 1-468, confidence 0.5-0.8\n\nLet me catalogue all invariants systematically:\n\n\nNow I'm going through the explicit invariants from the first section. The genre promise requires every story to be romantic fantasy with both arcs present and romance reaching at least a happy-for-now ending, affecting all subdomains. Internal consistency across all worldbuilding details and series continuity is non-negotiable, with failures in any artifact or chapter representing a critical breach. Character-related invariants are also coming into focus. I'm laying out more craft requirements: exposition needs to stay lean and integrated into scenes rather than standing alone, both romantic leads need genuine agency to drive their own choices, and no previously unestablished powers can suddenly solve problems. Then there's the structural foundation—I need an outline at the chapter level before drafting anything, specifying whose perspective, what they're after, and what stands in their way.\n\nBeyond that, I'm tracking continuity meticulously after each chapter, producing chapters in strict sequence so nothing gets out of order, and running every chapter through multiple passes: developmental feedback, line editing, consistency checks, and beta reads from different angles. Every revision gets documented with what prompted it.\n\nAt the verification stage, I'm comparing each POV character's voice across their chapters to ensure consistency, cross-referencing every factual detail against the continuity tracker, and enforcing a prose quality floor—rejecting any chapter with too many clichés, tell-not-show moments in critical scenes, or generic filler sentences.\n\nNow I'm laying out the remaining quality invariants: characters can't reference concepts that don't exist in their world, dialogue needs to feel natural with interruptions and contractions, pacing must alternate between tension levels, romance arcs have to escalate through proper stages, and the antagonist requires comprehensive motivation.\n\nMoving into originality requirements—all content must be entirely original with zero tolerance for plagiarism, style samples can only inform abstract patterns rather than reproduce content, and beta reading needs to include originality checks with citations for any resemblances.\n\nThen there's the process setup starting with parameterization for input fields like rough word count...\n\nNow I'm laying out the adversarial phase gates that need to happen before completing each creative phase, cross-referencing everything against prior artifacts to catch inconsistencies. When reference stories or style samples come in, I need to extract concrete style guidance from them. Every artifact also needs to trace back to its upstream dependencies—orphaned pieces indicate something went wrong.\n\nI'm also establishing that if something can't be completed, it has to be surfaced explicitly rather than silently failing. The system needs to deliver exactly what was specified in the concept phase without scope creep unless there's explicit re-approval. For series work, each book gets its own artifact directory while the series knowledge base lives at the root, and individual books can't directly reference each other's internal files. Content gets broken down into subcategory files for clarity. Finally, every foreshadowing element planted needs a corresponding resolution—one-to-one mapping with no loose threads.\n\nNow I'm looking at voice distinctiveness, where each POV character should be identifiable within just a few sentences. Prose sections need to maintain active tension throughout