ing out how to interleave fantasy and romance arcs so they reinforce each other, then charting tension's rise and fall across the pacing. Scene construction follows the Motivation-Reaction Unit pattern, where each beat flows from feeling into reflex into rational action. I'm also building an explicit ledger to track every foreshadowing plant and its payoff, and weaving 3-5 recurring symbols throughout to anchor the thematic core.\n\nBeyond that, I need to establish distinct voice fingerprints for each POV character with measurable parameters, track what each character knows versus what the reader knows to maintain dramatic tension, and audit every page to eliminate dead zones where tension drops. I'm charting the emotional arc granularly by chapter to ensure variety and escalation, then building a vulnerability escalation ladder to deepen character stakes.\n\nFor structure, I'm cataloguing each chapter's hook and closing technique to maintain variety, keeping 3-5 mystery boxes active throughout, analyzing the gap between dialogue and subtext, and scaffolding themes as arguments with competing answers that build toward resolution.\n\nI'm also anchoring each character's emotional expression to a specific sensory channel, then moving into the implicit invariants that weren't explicitly stated but are critical to the system design—the guide agent as the sole user-facing interface while other agents work autonomously, building for series production from the start, accepting just a story idea as input, and incorporating five independent beta reader perspectives.\n\nI need to capture that all critical and major findings from beta reading must be resolved before a chapter is complete, the system produces a Story Craft Profile during early planning phases documenting which tools are selected and why, and the series knowledge base operates append-mostly where prior established facts can't be contradicted without narrative justification and documentation.\n\nFor sequels, the system receives the series knowledge base, summaries of previous books, and any unresolved threads—and I need to decide what happens to each thread. POV transitions require clear motivation like a cliffhanger or time jump, the final prose must be publication-ready romantic fantasy quality, any artifact covering more than three distinct topics gets split into separate files, and every adversarial auditor needs to actively hunt for darlings to move into a killed-darlings file.\n\nWait, I'm noticing some overlap here. Looking back at the domain brief, several of these requirements are already captured in the invariants file, just phrased differently. Let me identify what's genuinely new: the guide agent requirements, the series-ready-from-day-one expectation, minimum viable input specifications, the five independent beta lenses, the craft profile process, the story-config.json artifact, the detailed subcategory decomposition rules, the series knowledge base lifecycle, and cross-book consistency constraints. Some of these expand on existing invariants, but several are distinct structural or behavioral requirements that need their own treatment.\n\nI should also flag the constraints that aren't covered anywhere: no simultaneous stories, re-readability seeding as a craft dimension, and the payoff spacing rule about maximum chapter gaps. These feel like they belong in the invariants because they're explicit normative statements from the brief, not just elaborations on what's already there.\n\nNow I'm extracting the core implicit invariants from the domain brief itself—things that are clearly required but not yet formalized. I'm looking at structural requirements like the guide being the sole interface and series-ready architecture from the start, behavioral constraints like minimum viable input being just a story idea and the append-mostly rule for the series knowledge base, workflow artifacts that must exist before execution, and quality standards around POV transitions and publication-ready prose. I'm assigning confidence levels based on how explicitly these are stated in the brief, ranging from 0.7 to 0.8.\n\nI'm also catching myself on a key insight: many of these rules aren't actually implicit at all—they're explicit in the brief itself, just stated as requirements rather than formal invariants. Since the brief is the authoritative requirements document, those explicit \"MUST\" statements deserve higher confidence scores of 0.8 to 0.9.\n\nNow I need to map out what belongs in the domain-model.json file and prepare the output structure, but I'm pausing to take another careful look at the Craft Toolbox items to make sure I'm capturing them correctly.\n\nThe invariants document makes it clear that craft tools become binding once selected for a story—they're conditionally enforced rather than always-on rules. That's an important distinction. They're explicitly documented, so I'm confident at 0.9, but I need to flag that they only apply after selection.\n\nTime to generate the full JSON inventory, numbering each entry from INV-001 onward and organizing them by their source and confidence level.\n\nStarting with the explicit invariants from Part 1, which carry the highest confidence since they're foundational rules the system must always follow. I'm cataloging them across behavioral, quality, and workflow categories—Genre Promise, Internal Consistency, Character Voice Distinctness, Earned Emotional Beats, Show Don't Tell, Chekhov's Gun, No Info-Dumping, Character Agency, No Deus Ex Machina, Outline Before Draft, Continuity Tracking, Sequential Chapter Production, Multi-Pass Review, and more.\n\nNow I'm moving into the verification and quality-assurance layer with Voice Consistency Verification, Continuity Verification Exhaustive, and Prose Quality Floor, then expanding into more specific quality gates like No Anachronisms, Dialogue Naturalism, Pacing Variation, Romance Arc Pacing, Antagonist Motivation, No Plagiarism, Transformative Influence Only, and Originality Self-Audit, plus workflow considerations around parameterization.\n\nContinuing through the structural and behavioral requirements — Adversarial Phase Gates, Input Consumption, Artifact Cross-References, No Silent Failures, Scope Fidelity, Series Artifact Isolation & Subcategory Decomposition, Foreshadowing Resolution Completeness, POV Voice Distinctiveness, Micro-Tension Continuity, Thematic Coherence, and Emotional State Variety round out the comprehensive quality and structural framework.\n\nNow moving into the Craft Toolbox section with conditional binding — Scene-Sequel Structure, Scene Value Shifts, Five Commandments Per Scene, Try-Fail Cycles, Tone Contract, Stakes Escalation, The Black Moment, Internal Romantic Resistance, and Subtext in Dialogue form the conditional quality and behavioral requirements that apply when relevant to the narrative structure.\n\nContinuing through the remaining craft tools — Sanderson's First Law for magic clarity, Kill Your Darlings for workflow discipline, Dual-Arc Interleave for structural balance, Tension Mapping, Scene-Sequel MRU, Foreshadowing Plant-Payoff Ledger, Symbolic Motif Weaving, POV Voice Fingerprint Verification, and Information Asymmetry Mapping all carry conditional application across quality, behavioral, and structural dimensions.\n\nNow I'm cataloging the next batch of specialized techniques: Micro-Tension Audit, Emotional Throughline Charting, Vulnerability Escalation Ladder, Chapter Hook-and-Close Catalogue, Mystery Box Inventory, Dialogue Subtext Gap Analysis, Thematic Argument Scaffolding, and Sensory Signature Anchoring — all quality-focused with conditional triggers. I'm also noting that the guide agent functions as the sole user-facing entry point based on the domain brief.\n\nLooking at the architectural requirements now: the system needs to be series-ready from the start, accept just a story idea as minimum input, incorporate five independent beta reader perspectives, generate a Story Craft Profile during concept work, maintain an append-mostly knowledge base with narratively justified changes, ensure sequels address all loose threads, motivate any POV shifts, meet publication-ready prose standards, and keep subcategory files independently loadable.\n\nCross-references between sections use relative paths, critical and major revision findings must be resolved before acceptance, only one story can be in production at a time, the Craft Toolbox requires five to eight tools per story, and a story-config.json artifact needs to be created before the pipeline launches. I'm noticing INV-078 about tool selection is actually documented in the invariants file itself, so that confidence level should probably be higher than initially marked.\n\nLooking at the exact language in the source material, it says tools \"SHOULD\" be selected rather than \"MUST,\" which indicates guidance rather than an absolute requirement—so 0.85 confidence feels right. But there's a deeper structural point here: the two-tier quality system itself, where invariants are always enforced but craft tools are selected per-story, is a meta-invariant about how the whole system operates. Once a craft tool gets selected for a particular story, it becomes binding and auditors enforce it, which is actually a very high-confidence structural rule.\n\nNow I'm looking at what the brief explicitly mandates. The story-config.json requirement is clearly a workflow gate before the pipeline launches, and \"Kill Your Darlings\" appears both as a craft tool option and as a hard mandate in the adversarial auditor section with \"MUST\" language—so that's a distinct invariant from the toolbox version, not just a selectable option.\n\nBut I need to check whether this kill-your-darlings mandate is already covered by the existing Adversarial Phase Gates invariant. Looking at what that captures, it covers cross-referencing against prior artifacts and blocking on critical findings, but it doesn't explicitly call out the kill-your-darlings check itself. So the brief is adding a specific requirement there that should be captured separately.\n\nLet me narrow down to the genuinely new invariants from the brief that aren't just restatements of existing ones: the guide agent as sole user-facing entry point with all others autonomous, series-ready architecture from day one even for single stories, the system producing a complete story from minimal input, and the five independent reader lenses requirement for beta reading.\n\nNow I'm working through the rest of the list to identify which ones are truly new constraints versus rephrasing existing ones. I see requirements around the Story Craft Profile artifact, the append-mostly series knowledge base with justified retcons, explicit disposition decisions for unresolved threads in sequels, motivated POV transitions, publication-ready prose quality, self-contained subcategory files, relative file paths for cross-references, beta finding resolution before chapter acceptance, sequential story production, and craft toolbox selection minimums.\n\nThe binding nature of selected tools, the story-config.json confirmation requirement, and the mandatory kill-your-darlings check in adversarial audits round out the full set. Let me organize these by type to see the distribution across behavioral, structural, and other categories. So the total comes to 81 items across all categories. Looking at the confidence distribution, I have 65 high-confidence entries from the explicit parts, 16 medium-confidence ones derived from the brief section, and no low-confidence items—which gives me a solid foundation for this analysis.\n\nNow I'm mapping each invariant to its corresponding subdomain across the fourteen categories, from user guidance through craft knowledge systems. This is going to be a substantial JSON structure to build out.",
