      "message": {
        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "Good — the status file doesn't exist yet (the directory was pre-created but empty). Now I have all the data I need. Let me design and write the pipeline architecture.",
        "refusal": null,
        "tool_calls": [
          {
            "id": "toolu_01A53wczgShVvMXrDrGEruPb",
            "type": "function",
            "function": {
              "name": "edit",
              "arguments": "{\"path\": \"/home/jakubs/repositories/ralph-orchestrator/.fractal-factory/architecture.json\", \"old_str\": \"{\\n  \\\"version\\\": 1,\\n  \\\"lastUpdated\\\": null,\\n  \\\"pipeline\\\": null,\\n  \\\"artifacts\\\": null,\\n  \\\"depth\\\": null\\n}\", \"new_str\": \"{\\n  \\\"version\\\": 1,\\n  \\\"lastUpdated\\\": \\\"2026-03-16T00:25:00Z\\\",\\n  \\\"pipeline\\\": {\\n    \\\"passes\\\": [\\n      {\\n        \\\"pass\\\": 0,\\n        \\\"name\\\": \\\"knowledge-curation\\\",\\n        \\\"displayName\\\": \\\"Meta-Knowledge Curation\\\",\\n        \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Curate accumulated meta-knowledge from prior factory runs into a task-relevant knowledge-brief.json. On cold start (first run), produce a minimal brief acknowledging no prior signals exist. On subsequent runs, distill craft-domain signals (prose patterns, worldbuilding consistency strategies, invariant enforcement lessons) into actionable guidance for downstream passes.\\\",\\n        \\\"entryCondition\\\": \\\"Always first — runs before any domain pass. Triggered unconditionally at pipeline start.\\\",\\n        \\\"exitCondition\\\": \\\"knowledge-brief.json written with coldStart flag set appropriately. Single-agent pass — no coordinator needed.\\\",\\n        \\\"coordinatorHint\\\": null,\\n        \\\"estimatedAgents\\\": 1,\\n        \\\"parallelizable\\\": false,\\n        \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Cold start for this run (first fantasy-writer session). Future runs will have craft-domain signals from the synthesis pass to curate. This pass is a no-op structurally but establishes the knowledge-brief.json contract for downstream consumers.\\\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \\\"pass\\\": 1,\\n        \\\"name\\\": \\\"discovery\\\",\\n        \\\"displayName\\\": \\\"Domain Discovery\\\",\\n        \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Scan the fantasy-writer domain to build a comprehensive domain model: 14 subdomains (10 sequential creative phases + 4 cross-cutting concerns), behavioral/quality/workflow/structural invariants, reusable infrastructure assets, and exemplar hierarchy patterns. Produces the domain-model.json that all subsequent passes consume.\\\",\\n        \\\"entryCondition\\\": \\\"knowledge-brief.json exists (Pass 0 complete). No other preconditions.\\\",\\n        \\\"exitCondition\\\": \\\"All four discovery specialists (domain-scanner, invariant-extractor, asset-auditor, exemplar-analyzer) report status=completed. domain-model.json contains subdomains, invariants, existingAssets, and exemplarPatterns arrays. Discovery coordinator confirms completeness.\\\",\\n        \\\"coordinatorHint\\\": \\\"fractal-factory-discovery-coordinator\\\",\\n        \\\"estimatedAgents\\\": 5,\\n        \\\"parallelizable\\\": true,\\n        \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Four specialists scan independent aspects: (1) domain-scanner extracts 14 subdomains from domain-brief.md, (2) invariant-extractor classifies 81 invariants into behavioral/quality/workflow/structural, (3) asset-auditor catalogs 26 reusable assets (7 shared-includes, 11 skills, 3 agent-templates, 4 tools, 1 MCP server), (4) exemplar-analyzer extracts 20 patterns from the fractal-factory exemplar family. All run in parallel under the coordinator. ALREADY COMPLETED in this run.\\\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \\\"pass\\\": 2,\\n        \\\"name\\\": \\\"analysis\\\",\\n        \\\"displayName\\\": \\\"Architecture Analysis\\\",\\n        \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Analyze the discovered domain model to determine three architectural decisions: (1) pipeline pass structure and re-entry rules for the produced fantasy-writer system, (2) shared artifact schemas and data flow contracts between agents, (3) hierarchy depth (depth-2 vs depth-3) per coordinator based on subdomain complexity. These three analyses are independent and inform the planning pass.\\\",\\n        \\\"entryCondition\\\": \\\"Pass 1 complete — domain-model.json exists with all four sections populated. Discovery coordinator status=completed.\\\",\\n        \\\"exitCondition\\\": \\\"All three analysis specialists (pipeline-architect, artifact-designer, depth-analyzer) report status=completed. architecture.json contains populated pipeline, artifacts, and depth sections.\\\",\\n        \\\"coordinatorHint\\\": \\\"fractal-factory-analysis-coordinator\\\",\\n        \\\"estimatedAgents\\\": 4,\\n        \\\"parallelizable\\\": true,\\n        \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Three independent analyses run in parallel: pipeline-architect designs the produced system's pass structure (this agent — designing the factory pipeline); artifact-designer maps the 30+ artifact types across 14 subdomains into schemas with read-modify-write contracts; depth-analyzer evaluates each subdomain's complexity against the maxDepth=3 budget and maxAgents=80 ceiling. The high complexity of 7 subdomains (worldbuilding, character-development, plotting, chapter-drafting, revision, beta-reading, adversarial-consistency) plus 81 invariants strongly suggests depth-3 is needed for creative phases. CURRENTLY IN PROGRESS.\\\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \\\"pass\\\": 3,\\n        \\\"name\\\": \\\"planning\\\",\\n        \\\"displayName\\\": \\\"Roster & Routing Planning\\\",\\n        \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Plan the complete agent roster (up to 80 agents), routing tables for every coordinator and the orchestrator, and golden test scenarios. Roster planning assigns agents to the 14 subdomains respecting hierarchy depth decisions. Routing planning defines the markdown routing tables with read/condition/action columns. Test planning creates scenarios covering the creative pipeline end-to-end, series production, adversarial gate failures, and craft-tool enforcement.\\\",\\n        \\\"entryCondition\\\": \\\"Pass 2 complete — architecture.json fully populated with pipeline, artifacts, and depth sections. All three analysis agents report status=completed.\\\",\\n        \\\"exitCondition\\\": \\\"roster.json contains all agents with assigned levels, parents, children, result codes, and artifact assignments. routing-tables.json or equivalent contains routing tables for every coordinator. test-scenarios.json contains golden test cases. Planning coordinator confirms completeness.\\\",\\n        \\\"coordinatorHint\\\": \\\"fractal-factory-planning-coordinator\\\",\\n        \\\"estimatedAgents\\\": 4,\\n        \\\"parallelizable\\\": false,\\n        \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Sequential dispatch required: roster-planner runs first (routing and tests depend on knowing agent names and hierarchy). Then routing-planner builds tables using the roster. Finally test-planner creates scenarios referencing both roster and routes. The 14-subdomain, 80-agent budget with depth-3 hierarchy makes this the most complex planning pass. Key decisions: how to allocate the agent budget across 10 sequential phases + 4 cross-cutting concerns, how many specialist writers vs. reviewers per creative phase, and how adversarial auditors integrate as sub-phase gates.\\\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \\\"pass\\\": 4,\\n        \\\"name\\\": \\\"execution\\\",\\n        \\\"displayName\\\": \\\"Prompt Writing & Infrastructure\\\",\\n        \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Write all agent prompt files (.agent.md) following the universal prompt template, and produce infrastructure files (bootstrap script, artifact schemas, skill stubs, golden test files). Uses a bounded writer→reviewer loop: prompt-writer produces prompts, prompt-reviewer adversarially checks for structural compliance, content quality, routing completeness, anti-laziness enforcement, and domain-specific correctness. Rejected prompts are re-written with reviewer feedback.\\\",\\n        \\\"entryCondition\\\": \\\"Pass 3 complete — roster.json, routing tables, and test scenarios all exist. Planning coordinator status=completed.\\\",\\n        \\\"exitCondition\\\": \\\"All agent prompts written and accepted by reviewer (or max retries reached with best-effort noted). Infrastructure files written. Execution coordinator confirms all roster entries have corresponding prompt files.\\\",\\n        \\\"coordinatorHint\\\": \\\"fractal-factory-execution-coordinator\\\",\\n        \\\"estimatedAgents\\\": 3,\\n        \\\"parallelizable\\\": false,\\n        \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Writer→reviewer loop with maxWriterReviewerRetries=10 (from context.json). For ~80 agents, this is the longest pass. The prompt-writer must handle diverse agent types: creative specialists (worldbuilders, character designers, chapter drafters), adversarial auditors (consistency checkers, craft enforcers), structural agents (continuity trackers, series KB managers), and simulation agents (5 beta reader lenses). The reviewer must enforce: pure-router purity for coordinators, ask_questions suppression for all non-guide agents, status.json contract compliance, and domain invariant awareness. Infrastructure includes the produced system's bootstrap script, artifact directory templates, and schema files for the 30+ artifact types.\\\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \\\"pass\\\": 5,\\n        \\\"name\\\": \\\"verification\\\",\\n        \\\"displayName\\\": \\\"Dual-Perspective Verification\\\",\\n        \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Validate all produced artifacts from two independent perspectives: (1) checklist-validator runs a structural and behavioral validation checklist — file existence, template compliance, routing table completeness, artifact schema coverage, invariant enforcement traceability; (2) audit-oracle evaluates from agent-as-function and fractal-workflow-eval perspectives — contract drift, architecture violations, routing purity violations, cross-reference integrity.\\\",\\n        \\\"entryCondition\\\": \\\"Pass 4 complete — all prompt files and infrastructure files written. Execution coordinator status=completed.\\\",\\n        \\\"exitCondition\\\": \\\"Both verification agents report status=completed. If critical issues found, they are logged in verification findings for gap-hunting to process. No verification failure blocks progression to gap-hunting — issues are carried forward.\\\",\\n        \\\"coordinatorHint\\\": \\\"fractal-factory-verification-coordinator\\\",\\n        \\\"estimatedAgents\\\": 3,\\n        \\\"parallelizable\\\": true,\\n        \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Two independent perspectives run in parallel under the coordinator. Given 81 invariants (21 behavioral, 33 quality, 13 workflow, 14 structural), the checklist-validator has substantial work tracing each invariant to its enforcement point in the produced prompts. The audit-oracle checks higher-level architecture: are all 14 subdomains covered, do routing tables form a complete DAG, do artifact contracts have matching producers and consumers, are adversarial gates actually adversarial (not rubber-stamping). The fantasy-writer domain's complexity makes this pass critical — the cross-cutting concerns (adversarial-consistency, series-knowledge, continuity-tracking, craft-knowledge) are easy to under-specify.\\\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \\\"pass\\\": 6,\\n        \\\"name\\\": \\\"gapHunting\\\",\\n        \\\"displayName\\\": \\\"Gap Hunting & Convergence\\\",\\n        \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Systematically search for gaps across 9 categories using 3 specialist hunters: (1) coverage-hunter checks subdomain coverage, invariant enforcement completeness, and routing completeness; (2) artifact-hunter checks artifact schema coverage, test scenario coverage, and cross-reference integrity; (3) infrastructure-hunter checks bootstrap completeness, documentation completeness, and meta-knowledge infrastructure. Findings trigger re-entry into earlier passes for repair.\\\",\\n        \\\"entryCondition\\\": \\\"Pass 5 complete — verification findings exist. Verification coordinator status=completed.\\\",\\n        \\\"exitCondition\\\": \\\"All three hunters report status=completed. Gap-report.json aggregated. If gaps-found=false (zero new items across all 9 categories), convergence achieved — proceed to delivery. If gaps-found=true, trigger re-entry per re-entry rules.\\\",\\n        \\\"coordinatorHint\\\": \\\"fractal-factory-gap-hunting-coordinator\\\",\\n        \\\"estimatedAgents\\\": 4,\\n        \\\"parallelizable\\\": true,\\n        \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Three specialist hunters run in parallel, each covering 3 of 9 gap categories. For the fantasy-writer domain, likely gap sources: (a) missing invariant enforcement — 81 invariants touching up to 47 agents each (SD-007 alone has 47 invariant touches) makes it likely some enforcement paths are missed; (b) craft-tool conditional invariants ('When selected: ...') need craft-profile-aware routing that may be under-specified; (c) series production paths (sequel handling, series KB updates, unresolved-threads disposition) are easy to miss on first pass; (d) 5 beta reader lenses need distinct structured-output schemas that may not all be specified. The maxGapCycles=10 allows generous iteration, but convergence should typically occur in 2-4 cycles for this domain.\\\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \\\"pass\\\": 7,\\n        \\\"name\\\": \\\"delivery\\\",\\n        \\\"displayName\\\": \\\"Packaging & Delivery\\\",\\n        \\\"purpose\\\": \\\"Package the complete fantasy-writer agent system into the target output directory (.fractal-factory/output/romantic-fantasy-writer), write architecture documentation and user guide, and produce a final delivery report with coverage statistics, quality metrics, outstanding items, and recommendations for future iterations.\\\",\\n        \\\"entryCondition\\\": \\\"Gap hunting converged (gaps-found=false) OR maxGapCycles reached. Gap-hunting coordinator status=completed with result indicating convergence or limit.\\\",\\n        \\\"exitCondition\\\": \\\"All delivery agents report status=completed. Output directory contains all prompt files, infrastructure files, documentation, and the delivery report. Delivery coordinator confirms package integrity.\\\",\\n        \\\"coordinatorHint\\\": \\\"fractal-factory-delivery-coordinator\\\",\\n        \\\"estimatedAgents\\\": 4,\\n        \\\"parallelizable\\\": false,\\n        \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Sequential: packager assembles the output directory first (copies and organizes all agent prompts, infrastructure files, artifact schemas into the romantic-fantasy-writer prefix structure), then documentation-writer produces architecture docs and a user guide (how to invoke the produced system, expected inputs/outputs, series production workflow), then report-writer summarizes the entire factory run including coverage statistics (invariants enforced/total, subdomains covered/total, agents produced/budget), quality metrics from verification, and any outstanding gap items. This is the terminal pass.\\\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \\\"reEntryRules\\\": [\\n      {\\n        \\\"id\\\": \\\"RE-001\\\",\\n        \\\"trigger\\\": \\\"Gap-hunting coordinator finds new items requiring architectural analysis (e.g., missing artifact schemas, incorrect depth assignments, pipeline pass structure issues)\\\",\\n        \\\"reEntryPass\\\": 2,\\n        \\\"resetPasses\\\": [2, 3, 4, 5, 6],\\n        \\\"maxReEntries\\\": 2,\\n        \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Heaviest re-entry — resets nearly the entire pipeline. Should be rare; typically only triggered if a fundamental architectural assumption was wrong (e.g., depth-2 is insufficient for a subdomain originally assessed as medium complexity). Deletes status.json for all agents in passes 2-6.\\\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \\\"id\\\": \\\"RE-002\\\",\\n        \\\"trigger\\\": \\\"Gap-hunting coordinator finds new items requiring roster or routing changes (e.g., missing agents for a subdomain, incomplete routing paths, missing test scenarios)\\\",\\n        \\\"reEntryPass\\\": 3,\\n        \\\"resetPasses\\\": [3, 4, 5, 6],\\n        \\\"maxReEntries\\\": 3,\\n        \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Moderate re-entry — adds or modifies agents in the roster and re-executes downstream. Most likely trigger: gap hunter discovers a subdomain aspect (e.g., series sequel handling, craft-tool conditional enforcement) that needs a dedicated agent not in the original roster. Routing tables are rebuilt to include new agents.\\\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \\\"id\\\": \\\"RE-003\\\",\\n        \\\"trigger\\\": \\\"Gap-hunting coordinator finds items requiring only prompt rewrites or infrastructure updates (e.g., missing invariant enforcement in existing prompts, incomplete artifact schemas, missing skill references)\\\",\\n        \\\"reEntryPass\\\": 4,\\n        \\\"resetPasses\\\": [4, 5, 6],\\n        \\\"maxReEntries\\\": 5,\\n        \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Lightest meaningful re-entry — the most common trigger. Typical causes: invariant enforcement gaps (81 invariants across 14 subdomains = many enforcement paths to check), missing cross-references between agent prompts, incomplete craft-tool conditional logic in auditor prompts. Only resets execution, verification, and gap-hunting. High maxReEntries because prompt-level fixes are cheap.\\\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \\\"id\\\": \\\"RE-004\\\",\\n        \\\"trigger\\\": \\\"Verification finds critical structural violations that invalidate prompt correctness (e.g., routing table references non-existent agents, artifact producer/consumer mismatch)\\\",\\n        \\\"reEntryPass\\\": 4,\\n        \\\"resetPasses\\\": [4, 5],\\n        \\\"maxReEntries\\\": 2,\\n        \\\"notes\\\": \\\"Verification-triggered re-entry — bypasses gap hunting. Only triggers on critical (not major/minor) findings from the verification pass. Resets execution and verification only; gap hunting runs fresh after the fix. Prevents the need for a full gap-hunting cycle when the issue is clearly identified by verification.\\\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \\\"convergence\\\": {\\n      \\\"maxGapCycles\\\": 10,\\n      \\\"convergenceSignal\\\": \\\"Gap-hunting coordinator aggregates all three specialist hunter reports and finds gaps-found=false — zero new items across all 9 categories (subdomain coverage, invariant enforcement, routing completeness, artifact coverage, test coverage, cross-reference integrity, bootstrap completeness, documentation completeness, meta-knowledge infrastructure).\\\",\\n      \\\"limitBehavior\\\": \\\"Proceed to delivery (Pass 7) with all outstanding gap items flagged in the delivery report. The report-writer categorizes unresolved gaps by severity and provides remediation guidance. No gap item blocks delivery — the produced system ships with known limitations documented.\\\",\\n      \\\"expectedCycles\\\": \\\"2-4 cycles expected for the fantasy-writer domain. First cycle likely catches invariant enforcement gaps (81 invariants × 14 subdomains = high surface area) and series-production edge cases. Second cycle catches cross-reference integrity issues from first-cycle prompt additions. Third cycle should be clean for most categories. Fourth cycle is insurance for the craft-tool conditional invariants (26 'When selected' invariants requiring craft-profile-aware routing).\\\",\\n      \\\"convergenceAccelerators\\\": [\\n        \\\"Gap hunters report declining item counts per cycle — if total new items drops below 5, convergence is likely on next cycle\\\",\\n        \\\"RE-003 (prompt-level fixes) is preferred over RE-002 (roster changes) when possible, to minimize blast radius\\\",\\n        \\\"Verification-triggered RE-004 handles critical issues inline without consuming a gap-hunting cycle\\\"\\n      ]\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"artifacts\\\": null,\\n  \\\"depth\\\": null\\n}\"}"
