        "reasoning_text": "Now I have a comprehensive understanding of the domain. Let me analyze each coordinator in the produced fantasy-writer system. I need to think about the PRODUCED system's coordinators, not the factory system's coordinators.\n\nLooking at the architecture pipeline, these are the factory passes. But the produced system has a different pipeline - it's the romantic fantasy fiction writing pipeline. Let me re-examine what the produced system's pipeline looks like.\n\nLooking at the pipeline passes, these describe the factory passes (discovery, analysis, planning, execution, verification, gapHunting, delivery). But the PRODUCED system - the romantic-fantasy-writer - will have its own pipeline of passes based on the 14 subdomains:\n\n\nNow I'm mapping out the creative phases of the produced system, which breaks down into nine major subdomains ranging from initial user input through concept development, worldbuilding, character work, and plotting, with varying complexity levels and agent counts assigned to each phase.\n\nI need to reconsider what I'm actually analyzing here—the depth analyzer's role is to determine which coordinators in the produced romantic-fantasy-writer system need depth-2 versus depth-3 configurations, and I should be looking at the creative writing pipeline described in the domain brief rather than the factory's pipeline passes. I need to shift focus to the produced system's internal structure—the romantic-fantasy-writer will have its own orchestrator managing coordinators for each major creative phase, with specialists underneath. Looking at the 14 subdomains, I'm mapping out which ones become coordinators: the Guide serves as an entry point, while Concept, Worldbuilding, and the other major phases each get their own coordinator to manage that stage of the creative pipeline.\n\nThe cross-cutting concerns like the adversarial system, series knowledge management, continuity tracking, and craft knowledge might not all need to be full coordinators—some could function as shared specialists that support multiple coordinators instead.\n\nNow I'm looking at which coordinators warrant a deeper organizational structure versus a flatter one. I'm checking this against a few key factors: how many direct specialists each needs, how many cross-cutting concerns they handle, whether they break into distinct subdomain groups, and what the exemplar pattern suggests. The exemplar uses depth-2 for phase coordinators, so that's not pushing me toward depth-3.\n\nLet me evaluate each coordinator starting with the concept development phase, which looks like it needs just two agents.\n\nFor worldbuilding, I'm seeing more complexity—the world bible breaks into five distinct categories like geography, magic, politics, culture, and history. Even though the initial estimate was three specialists, those five subcategories suggest I might need more agents than the threshold. But five specialists still falls under the eight-agent limit for depth-2, so I need to think about whether the subdomain fragmentation itself justifies going deeper.\n\nActually, I'm realizing I should be clearer about what \"direct specialists\" means in this framework. If worldbuilding needs one specialist per world-bible subcategory plus an adversarial auditor to catch consistency issues, that's roughly six agents total—still within the depth-2 range. So the question becomes whether the structure benefits from adding an extra coordination layer or if depth-2 can handle it. Looking at the Character Coordinator, I see four specialists with four cross-cutting concerns—that triggers the depth-3 criterion, but with only four people total, it's a tight call. For Plotting, I'm starting to map out the structure framework selector and chapter roles, but I need to weigh whether adding sub-coordinators actually helps or just bloats the agent count when I'm already constrained by the 80-agent budget. outliner (1)\n- Dual-arc timeline builder (1)\n- Tension map creator (1)\n- Plot auditor (1)\n- Total: ~5 specialists. Cross-cutting: 4 (adversarial, craft-knowledge, continuity, reader-experience)\n- Cross-cutting > 3 triggers. Subdomain groups could be 3-4.\n- **Two criteria may trigger (cross-cutting > 3, possibly subdomain groups > 3).**\n\n### Style Coordinator  \n- Style analyzer (1)\n- Style guide writer (1)\n- Style auditor (1)\n- Total: ~3 specialists. Cross-cutting: 3.\n- **Depth-2.** Nothing triggers.\n\n### Chapter Drafting Coordinator\nThe chapter drafting work is the most complex with 47 invariants touching it. I need a drafter for each chapter, plus a continuity checker working inline, a craft enforcer, and a chapter auditor—roughly 4-5 specialists total. The cross-cutting concerns (adversarial, continuity, craft-knowledge, multi-POV) hit 4 dimensions, which triggers depth-3, though the specialist count itself stays modest. The artifact surface is substantial with chapters/{N}/draft.md and chapters/{N}/metadata.json files.\n\n### Revision Coordinator\nFour specialists handle revision work: a developmental editor, line editor, copy editor, and revision auditor. Cross-cutting concerns are limited to continuity and craft-knowledge, so nothing triggers depth escalation.\n\n### Beta-Reading Coordinator\nFive distinct reader lenses (romance, fantasy, craft, sensitivity, originality) plus a synthesis agent and possibly an auditor gives 6-7 specialists. The five lenses create five subdomain groups, which exceeds the threshold of 3 and triggers depth-3 architecture.\n\n### Polish Coordinator\nThree specialists handling proofreading, summary generation, and series KB promotion with just one cross-cutting concern keeps this at depth-2.\n\n### Cross-cutting Coordinators\nAdversarial Consistency might not be a coordinator itself but rather a pattern where each phase has its own adversarial agent, though if structured as a coordinator it would have 2 agents at depth-2. Series Knowledge Management and Continuity Tracking each have 2 agents, both at depth-2.\n\n### Craft Knowledge Systems\nThis one's more complex with 3 agents serving four creative phases—plotting, chapter-drafting, revision, and beta-reading. I could expand it to track foreshadowing, information asymmetry, emotional throughlines, mystery boxes, and symbolic motifs, but I'm estimating it stays at 3 agents and depth-2 even with those expanded concerns.\n\nNow I need to step back and think about the produced system itself—the novel-writing pipeline. Rather than thinking about the factory structure, I should focus on what the actual creative phases look like: Concept Development, Worldbuilding, and the phases that follow.\n\nLooking at the full pipeline, I see nine distinct creative phases: Character Development, Plotting & Outlining, Style Calibration, Chapter Drafting, Revision (with its three sub-passes), Beta Reading (with five parallel lenses), and Polish & Delivery. The real question is which of these phases genuinely warrant depth-3 complexity in the produced system's architecture.\n\nI'm realizing I need to reframe this entirely—the Fractal Factory depth decisions are about the produced agent family structure, not the factory itself. So I should group these creative phases into what would become the produced system's coordinators, and Option A suggests giving each of the nine sequential phases its own coordinator, with the four cross-cutting concerns woven in as shared resources.\n\nBut looking at the actual pipeline and the 80-agent budget constraint, I think grouping related phases makes more sense: Foundation (concept, worldbuilding, character), Design (plotting, style), Production (chapter drafting), and Quality (revision, beta-reading, polish). This gives me a cleaner structure to work with as I figure out the depth allocation. leaves 78 for coordinators + specialists\n\nIf 10 coordinators (one per phase): 10 coordinators + 68 specialists. Some phases need more specialists than others.\n\nLet me look at the pipeline architecture more carefully. The architecture.json only shows the FACTORY pipeline right now, but the artifact-designer section maps out domain-specific artifacts that reveal how the produced system is actually structured across its phases.\n\nTracing through the artifacts, I can see the full pipeline: concept phase produces story and craft profiles, worldbuilding generates the world bible, character phase creates character profiles and romance arcs, plotting produces structure and chapter outlines, style phase creates the style guide, drafting generates chapter drafts with metadata, revision phase produces developmental and line edits with revised chapters, beta phase collects feedback across multiple lenses with synthesis documents, polish phase finalizes chapters and summaries, and delivery produces the final report. There are also cross-cutting concerns like audit reports, continuity tracking, and various tracking documents for foreshadowing and emotional throughlines.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the coordinator structure. With 80 agents total and needing to be conservative about depth-3 structures, I should map each phase to a coordinator and carefully decide which ones need sub-coordinators based on complexity and specialist count.\n\nFor the Concept Coordinator, I'm keeping it at depth-2 since three direct specialists plus cross-cutting concerns fit cleanly without subdivision. The Worldbuilding Coordinator is trickier—it has six specialists and five distinct subdomain areas like geography and magic systems, but I think keeping it at depth-2 is still workable since the specialists can handle those domains directly without needing intermediate sub-coordinators.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the Worldbuilding Coordinator more carefully. The rule is clear: if subdomain groups exceed three, that triggers a depth-3 recommendation. I have five subdomain groups here, so mechanically that should push toward depth-3, even though six specialists feels manageable. Let me apply the decision rule strictly first, then factor in the budget constraints.\n\nFor the Character Coordinator, I'm seeing four cross-cutting concerns, which crosses the threshold and triggers depth-3. The Plotting Coordinator is starting to look similar—I need to count up the specialists and cross-cutting concerns to see if it also hits a trigger.\n\nLooking at Plotting, I've got six direct specialists and four cross-cutting concerns, plus four subdomain groups. That's two triggers firing, so depth-3 is definitely recommended there. Style Coordinator is lighter—only three specialists, three cross-cutting concerns, and two subdomain groups, so it stays at depth-2.\n\nNow for Chapter Drafting, this is where things get dense. I'm seeing 47 invariants, six to seven specialists, and four cross-cutting concerns across three subdomain groups. The cross-cutting threshold is met, which points to depth-3. But I'm realizing the per-chapter iteration pattern here is different—the coordinator loops through chapters and dispatches the same specialist set each time, rather than expanding the team. That might actually be better handled as a sequential loop rather than adding depth.\n\nMoving to Revision, this is much leaner: four specialists, two cross-cutting concerns, three subdomain groups. No triggers for depth-3, so depth-2 is the right call.\n\nBeta-Reading has six specialists and I'm checking the cross-cutting concerns now. The Polish Coordinator has just two subdomain groups with no depth-3 triggers, so depth-2 is appropriate. Now I'm considering the cross-cutting service coordinators—these four subdomains likely don't warrant standalone coordinators but instead get embedded as shared specialists within the phase coordinators themselves. The adversarial auditors are an interesting case: they could either be distributed as per-phase gates or consolidated into a single coordinator that manages a pool of auditors deployed across the creative phases.\n\nLooking at the domain model more carefully, the auditor pattern is described as a sub-phase gate embedded within each creative phase rather than a centralized function, which suggests there's no separate adversarial coordinator—each phase coordinator handles its own auditing gate. The same logic applies to continuity tracking and craft knowledge systems, which are better implemented as shared skills that phase coordinators invoke on demand. Series knowledge management might be the exception here, potentially warranting its own coordinator to manage the series knowledge base lifecycle.\n\nSo the actual structure would be nine phase coordinators handling concept through polish, plus an orchestrator to route between them and a guide as the user entry point. Cross-cutting concerns get handled through embedded auditors, shared specialists, and a series KB manager. That's eleven agents dedicated to coordination and infrastructure, leaving about seventy agents for the actual creative work across all the phases.\n\nNow I'm checking which phases warrant a third layer of coordinators. Worldbuilding, character work, plotting, chapter drafting, and beta reading all hit the threshold—either they have more than three subdomain groups or more than three cross-cutting concerns. Concept and style don't meet those criteria, so they'd stay at depth-two. But adding depth-three coordinators to five different phases would consume a significant chunk of that remaining budget, so I need to see if that's even feasible. For Character, I'm looking at a depth-3 structure with 2 sub-coordinators handling core characters versus ensemble, supported by 4 specialists covering protagonist, romance, supporting cast, and voice—that's 8 agents total, a 2-agent increase from depth-2. Plotting follows the same pattern with 9 agents split between structural and chapter-level design, also costing 2 more agents than the simpler approach.\n\nNow for Drafting, I'm organizing it similarly with creative writing and quality integration as sub-coordinators managing the actual writing and continuity work across 5 specialists, bringing the total to 9 agents. Beta Reading uses a comparable structure with reader lenses and synthesis as sub-coordinators, coordinating 5 specialized readers plus a synthesizer for a total of 9 agents as well.\n\nBut I'm realizing some of these depth-3 structures are overcomplicated. Having a sub-coordinator just for the synthesizer in beta reading doesn't make sense, and in worldbuilding, 2-3 specialists under a sub-coordinator doesn't justify the added layer. I should only use depth-3 when there are genuinely many specialists (8+), when they naturally cluster into distinct groups, or when the sub-coordinators would handle meaningfully different routing patterns. Looking at worldbuilding specifically, the 5 world-bible areas are distinct but follow similar workflows—a single coordinator can handle routing without needing sub-coordinators, so depth-3 adds unnecessary overhead here.\n\nCharacter has only 4 cross-cutting concerns and 5 specialists, which a single coordinator can manage fine. Plotting is stronger—4 cross-cutting concerns plus 4 subdomain groups with genuinely different workflows for structure selection, arc design, outlining, and scene beats. Chapter Drafting is the clearest case because it's the most invariant-heavy (47 invariants) with complex per-chapter iteration cycles that need sophisticated routing for both creative flow and quality integration. Beta Reading is just a simple fan-out/fan-in pattern with 5 reader lenses plus synthesis, so depth-3 would be wasteful overhead.\n\nI'm settling on a selective approach: only use depth-3 where the organizational complexity truly justifies it, and stick with depth-2 when specialist count stays at 8 or fewer. Chapter Drafting has the strongest case with its 47 invariants and dual-flow routing needs, followed by Plotting with its 41 invariants and meaningful structural design split, while Beta Reading's straightforward pattern doesn't need the extra layer.\n\nNow I'm running through the budget implications across different depth scenarios, starting with a baseline where everything stays at depth-2. So that brings us to 58 agents total, leaving about 22 agents of headroom within the 80-agent budget. I'm exploring whether to add depth-3 sub-coordinators to the most complex stages—drafting, plotting, and beta reading would each need 2 additional agents, totaling 6 more and bringing us to 64. Even if I went deeper and added sub-coordinators across all five major stages, we'd only hit 68 agents, still comfortably under the limit.\n\nBut I shouldn't just maximize depth because I can—I need to be strategic about where it actually adds value. Let me think through the specialist breakdown more carefully, being more generous with counts where the domain complexity genuinely warrants it. Looking at each stage, I'm estimating how many specialized roles each coordinator really needs to handle the work effectively. So I'm at 57 agents total with 23 slots remaining in my budget. That gives me room to add depth-3 sub-coordinators where they'd have the most impact. Chapter drafting is the strongest candidate—it's handling 47 invariants and 4 cross-cutting concerns with per-chapter iteration, so splitting it into creative and quality-gate sub-coordinators makes sense.\n\nPlotting also justifies depth-3 with its 41 invariants and distinct structural versus per-chapter design activities. Beta reading rounds out my depth-3 additions, leveraging its 5 reader lens types with separate coordination for panel management and synthesis.\n\nActually, I'm reconsidering—the fan-out for beta reading is straightforward enough that I can handle it at depth-2. Let me be more disciplined about this. I'll reserve depth-3 for Chapter Drafting and Plotting, which have the highest invariant counts and the most complex routing patterns. Worldbuilding, despite its 5 subdomain groups, follows a consistent workflow across each area, so a single coordinator can manage the routing without needing sub-coordinators.\n\nNow I'm checking the budget: adding 2 sub-coordinators each to Drafting and Plotting brings me to 61 agents total, which stays comfortably under the 80-agent limit.\n\nBut wait—I should double-check my decision rule. The criteria say to recommend depth-3 if ANY criterion triggers it and the max depth allows it, then adjust downward only if I exceed budget. So technically I should be recommending depth-3 more broadly, but let me verify this against the actual constraints before committing.\n\nActually, rereading the task, the key word is \"needs\"—I should be analyzing what each coordinator genuinely requires, not just mechanically applying rules. Drafting and Plotting both have substantial complexity with their invariant counts and cross-cutting concerns, plus they have distinct work streams that benefit from sub-coordination. Worldbuilding, despite triggering the criteria, doesn't really need that extra layer—5 specialists is manageable at depth-2.\n\nCharacter and Beta Reading are similar: they technically trigger depth-3, but the actual workload and workflow patterns suggest a single coordinator can handle them effectively. This keeps the system lean while still using depth-3 where the routing complexity genuinely justifies it.\n\nBut wait, I'm second-guessing myself against the stated process. The rules say to mechanically apply the criteria first, which would push everything to depth-3, then use the budget check to trim back. Let me follow that order properly.\n\nApplying the criteria mechanically gives me five workflows at depth-3: Worldbuilding, Character, Plotting, Drafting, and Beta Reading. The budget check shows I'm at around 68 agents with all five, which stays under the 80-agent limit, so no reductions are actually needed.\n\nNow I'm working through the sub-coordinator structure for each depth-3 workflow, starting with how to organize Worldbuilding into its component layers.\n\nFor Worldbuilding, I'm splitting it into physical-world and systems-world sub-coordinators, each with their own builders, plus an auditor at the main level—that gives me 9 total roles. Character follows a similar pattern with core-characters and ensemble sub-coordinators managing four specialists plus an auditor, totaling 8 roles. For Plotting, I'm setting up structural-design and chapter-design sub-coordinators to handle the different phases of plot development.\n\nNow I'm continuing with the remaining modules: Drafting gets its own two sub-coordinators for the creative and quality sides, plus an auditor, also hitting 9 roles. Beta Reading branches into a reader-panel coordinator managing five different reader types, with a synthesis coordinator starting to take shape underneath.\n\nActually, I'm realizing the synthesis coordinator with just one specialist feels inefficient, so I'm regrouping the readers by their lens—genre perspectives in one sub-coordinator and craft-focused readers in another—while keeping the synthesizer as a direct report to the main coordinator. This keeps the structure balanced at 9 roles while organizing readers by how they evaluate the work.\n\nNow I'm tallying up the full agent count: the Orchestrator and Guide at the top level, then Concept with its coordinator and three specialists.\n\nLooking at the deeper modules, I'm mapping out Worldbuilding, Character, Plotting, Style, Drafting, Revision, Beta Reading, and Polish—each with their own coordinator structures and varying depths. I'm also factoring in five cross-cutting shared agents that handle continuity, knowledge management, and craft elements like foreshadowing and symbolism across the whole system.\n\nThe total comes to 68 agents, which keeps us comfortably under the 80-agent budget. I'm noting that the depth-2 phases have 4 coordinators while the depth-3 phases have 5, and I need to reconsider how the cross-cutting concerns fit into the architecture—whether they're embedded as auditors within each phase or handled as shared specialists at a higher level.\n\nLooking at the actual system design, I see that the continuity tracker, series knowledge base manager, and the three craft specialists (foreshadowing, emotional throughline, and symbolic motif) operate as shared agents invoked across multiple coordinators rather than sitting under a single coordinator. That's 5 additional agents to account for, bringing the grand total to around 28 agents across the orchestrator, guide, and all coordinators combined.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering whether the exemplar pattern actually constrains the produced system's depth. The exemplar uses depth-2 coordinators, but that's the factory itself—not necessarily prescriptive for a more complex domain like creative writing with 14 subdomains and 81 invariants. The exemplar pattern doesn't strongly suggest the produced system needs to match that depth, so I'll mark that criterion as \"No\" across the board.\n\nWith that settled, I'm ready to generate the architecture.json update and status files.",
