        "reasoning_text": "Now I have a comprehensive picture of the architecture. Let me plan the complete roster. Let me build the roster systematically:\n\n## Agent Count Plan\n\nFrom architecture.json:\n- 1 orchestrator\n- 1 guide\n- 9 phase coordinators (concept, worldbuilding, character, plotting, style, drafting, revision, beta-reading, polish)\n- 10 sub-coordinators (for 5 depth-3 coordinators)\n- Specialists per coordinator:\n  - concept (depth-2): 3 specialists (concept-developer, craft-profile-selector, concept-auditor)\n  - worldbuilding (depth-3): 6 specialists split across 2 sub-coordinators\n    - physical-world: geography-builder, culture-builder, history-builder\n    - systems-world: magic-system-designer, political-structure-builder\n    - plus a shared worldbuilding-auditor\n  - character (depth-3): 5 specialists split across 2 sub-coordinators\n    - core-characters: protagonist-profiler, romance-arc-designer\n    - ensemble: supporting-cast-developer, character-voice-designer\n    - plus character-auditor\n  - plotting (depth-3): 6 specialists split across 2 sub-coordinators\n    - structural-design: structure-selector, dual-timeline-architect\n\n, tension-mapper\n    - chapter-design: chapter-outliner, scene-beat-designer\n    - plus plotting-auditor\n  - style (depth-2): 3 specialists handling analysis, guide-writing, and auditing\n  - drafting (depth-3): 5 specialists split across 2 sub-coordinators\n    - creative-writing: chapter-drafter, pov-voice-maintainer\n    - quality-integration: continuity-integrator, craft-enforcer\n    - plus drafting-auditor\n  - revision (depth-2): 4 specialists covering developmental, line, copy editing and auditing\n  - beta-reading (depth-3): 7 specialists split across 2 sub-coordinators\n    - genre-lens: romance-reader, fantasy-reader\n    - craft-lens: craft-reader, sensitivity-reader, originality-reader\n    - plus beta-synthesizer and beta-reading-auditor\n  - polish (depth-2): 3 specialists for proofreading, summary generation, and series promotion\n\n- 5 shared cross-cutting specialists handling continuity, series knowledge, foreshadowing, emotional throughlines, and symbolic motifs\n\nNow I'm looking at the actual artifact writer agent names more carefully—they're structured differently than my coordinator breakdown, with dedicated agents for concept development, worldbuilding, character development, plot architecture, and style calibration.\n\nContinuing through the full pipeline, I see there are specialized beta readers for romance, fantasy, craft, sensitivity, and originality concerns, plus agents for developmental editing, line editing, copy editing, chapter revision, synthesis, polishing, delivery assembly, and auditing.\n\nNow I'm working through how the depth-3 coordinators map to their sub-specialists—worldbuilding splits into physical world, systems world, and an auditing layer, while I'm trying to figure out whether the adversarial auditor sits at the coordinator level or embedded within each phase. Looking at the revision, beta-reading, and polish phases, I'm seeing 4, 7, and 3 specialists respectively. Now I'm double-checking the overall architecture to make sure the specialist counts align properly across all the coordinator roles and their auditors. Finishing up the style specialists with the analyzer, guide-writer, and auditor. Then I'm mapping out the remaining modules—drafting has five specialists covering creative and quality aspects, revision brings in four editors at different levels, beta-reading expands to seven with genre and craft readers plus synthesis and auditing roles, and polish wraps up with three final specialists handling proofreading and delivery.\n\nNow I'm noticing some overlap between the shared specialists I listed and what the artifact writers actually reference—the craft-tracker seems to handle multiple responsibilities like foreshadowing, emotional throughlines, and symbolic motifs that I'd initially separated out. I need to reconcile whether these should stay consolidated under craft-tracker or split into individual specialists. Looking at the artifact writers more carefully, I'm seeing that romantic-fantasy-writer-craft-tracker and romantic-fantasy-writer-chapter-drafter are the ones actually writing these interconnected artifacts—foreshadowing-ledger, mystery-box-inventory, emotional-throughline, and symbolic-motif all flow between them. So the five cross-cutting agents I outlined might need their names adjusted to match what's actually happening in the artifact data, or I need to verify whether these are truly separate agents or consolidated roles. I'm reconsidering the agent structure — the artifact schemas show a consolidated approach with just 3 shared agents, which is more practical than the original 5. Let me verify this against the cross-cutting agents section to make sure I'm reading the architecture correctly. I'm noticing a discrepancy between the architecture's five shared specialists and what the artifact schemas actually use—the last three are all labeled as \"craft-tracker\" in the schemas, so I should consolidate those into a single agent. That brings me down to three shared agents: continuity-tracker, series-kb-manager, and craft-tracker. But now I'm seeing another inconsistency—the artifact writers reference a \"romantic-fantasy-writer-delivery-assembler\" that doesn't appear in the polish coordinator's specialist list, which instead has proofreader, summary-generator, and series-kb-promoter. I need to figure out where this delivery-assembler fits and whether it's actually a separate role or something that's been renamed. -assembler (writes delivery-report.json, packages everything)\n\nAnd \"series-kb-promoter\" might be the series-kb-manager (shared agent, not a polish-specific specialist).\n\nSo the polish coordinator has 3 specialists: the polisher handling final text, the summary-generator for chapter summaries, and the delivery-assembler for packaging. The se