embly, followed by three cross-cutting specialists who track continuity, manage the series knowledge base, and monitor craft consistency throughout.\n\nLet me verify the total count across all these layers. I'm tallying up the guide, orchestrator, coordinators at various levels, and all the specialists across concept, worldbuilding, character, plotting, style, drafting, revision, beta-reading, and polish phases. That comes to 66 agents total, which fits comfortably under the 80-agent budget and aligns closely with the 68 the architecture originally estimated.\n\nNow I need to figure out where the auditors fit in this structure. The auditors for depth-3 coordinators should sit directly under the coordinator since they're auditing the entire phase, not nested under sub-coordinators. For the worldbuilding, character, plotting, drafting, and beta-reading phases, the auditors should be specialists reporting directly to their respective coordinators rather than being sub-coordinators themselves. I also need to map out the parent-child relationships for the shared agents.\n\nThe shared agents that multiple coordinators invoke should have the orchestrator as their parent since no single coordinator owns them. Let me finalize the roster with auditors as direct children of their depth-3 coordinators and shared specialists under the orchestrator. Then I'll work through the result codes for the creative writers.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the status codes for each role in the system. Specialists have outcomes like `completed`, `blocked`, and `revision-needed` based on auditor feedback. Auditors track whether they passed the phase gate or found issues. Coordinators at both levels report `complete`, `blocked`, or `revision-loop` depending on whether their children succeeded and passed auditing. Sub-coordinators add a `partial` state for when some specialists finish but not all.\n\nAt the top level, the orchestrator reports `delivered`, `delivered-with-gaps`, or `failed` based on whether the entire book production succeeded. The guide doesn't use result codes since it's directly invoked by the user.\n\nNow I need to reconcile the artifact writer names from the architecture design with the actual roster agent names—the worldbuilding role was originally named as a single writer but the depth decisions split it into five separate specialists like geography-builder and culture-builder, so I need to figure out whether to keep it unified or align with that split. I'm seeing the same pattern repeat for plotting—the artifact consolidates multiple specialists into one writer, but the depth analysis breaks them into focused roles. The structure-selector handles the overall plot framework, the dual-arc-builder manages the intertwined narrative threads, the tension-mapper orchestrates pacing and stakes, the chapter-outliner breaks the story into manageable sections, and the scene-beat-designer handles granular beat-by-beat execution.\n\nFor style, it's simpler—the style-analyzer examines reference works to understand tone and voice, then the style-guide-writer codifies those findings into a usable guide.\n\nNow I'm realizing the auditors need to be distributed across each phase rather than centralized, so each coordinator gets its own embedded auditor to validate work within that specific domain.\n\nLet me verify the specialist count for each phase: concept has three (developer, craft-profile selector, auditor), worldbuilding has six (geography, culture, history, magic system, political structure, plus auditor), and character is building out with the protagonist profiler, romance arc designer, supporting cast developer, and voice designer—I should finish tallying these to make sure the roster is balanced. I'm tallying up the complete roster—42 specialists across all creative domains, plus 3 shared roles, 10 sub-coordinators, 9 coordinators, and 1 orchestrator with a guide, bringing me to 66 agents total with 14 slots of breathing room under the 80-agent budget. Now I need to map out the coordinator pass assignments since the 8-pass architecture needs to align with how the creative coordinators will operate.\n\nThe produced system has its own 10-phase pipeline—from initial input through concept, worldbuilding, character work, plotting, style calibration, drafting, revision, beta reading, and final polish—and I should use these phase names to indicate where each agent operates in the roster's pass field rather than referencing the factory's internal passes.\n\nFor agents that work across multiple phases or handle orchestration, I'll mark them as \"cross-cutting\" or \"all\", while the user-facing guide gets null since it sits outside the pipeline entirely.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the naming convention—the agents need full qualified names like \"romantic-fantasy-writer-concept-coordinator\" to avoid collisions, with sub-coordinators and specialists following the same prefix pattern.\n\nGenerating the complete roster with Python to ensure valid JSON...\n\nActually, I need to reconsider the distinction between \"craft-profile-selector\" and \"concept-developer\"—they handle different responsibilities in the concept phase, where the selector determines which craft techniques apply and the developer builds the initial story concept, so both should exist in the roster.\n\nFor the polish phase, there's no auditor needed since the architecture only specifies one cross-cutting concern, so the polish-coordinator and polisher can work directly with the delivery-assembler. The beta-reading phase is different though—it needs a dedicated auditor as a direct child of the beta-reading-coordinator to oversee all the reader lenses and synthesize their feedback.\n\nNow I'm tracking down where the chapter-reviser agent fits in the hierarchy, since it appears across multiple revision report artifacts. I need to map out the reads and writes for each agent based on what I've extracted so far.\n\nLooking at the revision workflow more carefully, the three editors (developmental, line, copy) each produce reports, and then the chapter-reviser reads all those reports plus the beta synthesis to actually apply the edits and generate the revised chapters. That's five specialists under revision-coordinator, not four—unless the chapter-reviser is meant to be something different or sits elsewhere in the structure. the revision-auditor is definitely one of the 4 specialists, so I need to figure out who actually produces the revised chapter text. The copy-editor seems like the logical candidate since it's the final editing pass, though it's a bit unusual for copy-editing to generate the full revised markdown rather than just flagging corrections. Alternatively, maybe the chapter-reviser is a separate coordinating role that applies all the edits, or perhaps I'm misunderstanding how the workflow actually chains together. I'm realizing the cleanest approach is to make this a sequential pipeline where each of the three editors—developmental, line, and copy—not only produces their report but also passes along a progressively refined draft, with the revision-auditor then validating the final output against all the reports.\n\nBut I'm noticing the artifact spec shows the chapter-reviser writing the final revised.md, not the copy-editor, so I should probably add chapter-reviser as a fifth specialist in the revision phase rather than trying to fold that responsibility into one of the editors. That's one extra agent, but it keeps the architecture cleaner and aligns with what the artifact designer intended. Looking at the beta-synthesis references though, it seems the chapter-reviser might also be responsible for applying beta feedback, which means it straddles both the revision and post-revision phases.\n\nLet me settle on this: the chapter-reviser is a specialist under the revision-coordinator that handles the initial pass through the three revision reports to produce revised.md, and then the orchestrator can re-invoke i