      'gen_ai.output.messages': '[{"role":"assistant","parts":[{"type":"reasoning","content":"Let me start by reading the key input artifacts to understand the agent system I need to design routing tables for."}],"finish_reason":"stop"},{"role":"assistant","parts":[{"type":"tool_call","id":"toolu_01Vv57zuW29DwveLbVuKP6CU","name":"report_intent","arguments":{"intent":"Reading routing inputs"}}],"finish_reason":"stop"},{"role":"assistant","parts":[{"type":"tool_call","id":"toolu_01PmYbokELD55UNZJQTqtZdm","name":"view","arguments":{"path":"/home/jakubs/repositories/ralph-orchestrator/.fractal-factory"}}],"finish_reason":"stop"},{"role":"assistant","parts":[{"type":"tool_call","id":"toolu_01Q3QfVX4Hj2cyeV2QRVTAyw","name":"view","arguments":{"path":"/home/jakubs/repositories/ralph-orchestrator/.fractal-factory/context.json"}}],"finish_reason":"stop"},{"role":"assistant","parts":[{"type":"tool_call","id":"toolu_018usqx31JKuUGy53a7vtBXV","name":"view","arguments":{"path":"/home/jakubs/repositories/ralph-orchestrator/.fractal-factory/roster.json","forceReadLargeFiles":true}}],"finish_reason":"stop"},{"role":"assistant","parts":[{"type":"tool_call","id":"toolu_01KxJXtJLKvDn9PoxYLVRXA1","name":"view","arguments":{"path":"/home/jakubs/repositories/ralph-orchestrator/.fractal-factory/architecture.json","forceReadLargeFiles":true}}],"finish_reason":"stop"},{"role":"assistant","parts":[{"type":"reasoning","content":"Now I have a comprehensive understanding of the system. Let me analyze the roster and architecture to design routing tables for every coordinator, sub-coordinator, and the orchestrator.\\n\\nLet me catalog the routing agents:\\n\\n**Orchestrator:**\\n- `romantic-fantasy-writer` (A-002) — routes 9 coordinators + 3 cross-cutting specialists\\n\\n**Coordinators (9):**\\n1. `concept-coordinator` (A-003) — depth-2, children: concept-developer, craft-profile-selector, concept-auditor\\n2. `worldbuilding-coordinator` (A-004) — depth-3, children: physical-world-coordinator, systems-world-coordinator, worldbuilding-auditor\\n\\n\\n3. `character-coordinator` (A-005) — depth-3, children: core-characters-coordinator, ensemble-coordinator, character-auditor\\n4. `plotting-coordinator` (A-006) — depth-3, children: structural-design-coordinator, chapter-design-coordinator, plotting-auditor\\n5. `style-coordinator` (A-007) — depth-2, children: style-analyzer, style-guide-writer, style-auditor\\n6. `drafting-coordinator` (A-008) — depth-3, children: creative-writing-coordinator, quality-integration-coordinator, drafting-auditor\\n7. `revision-coordinator` (A-009) — continuing through the remaining coordinators with depth-2, handling developmental editing, line editing, copy editing, chapter revision, and auditing\\n8. `beta-reading-coordinator` (A-010) — depth-3, bringing in genre and craft perspectives plus synthesis and auditing\\n9. `polish-coordinator` (A-011) — depth-2, handling final polishing, summary generation, and delivery assembly\\n\\nNow I\'m mapping out the sub-coordinators that support the main ones, starting with world-building components for physical and systems elements, then character-specific coordinators for protagonists and ensemble members.\\n\\nContinuing through structural and chapter design coordinators that handle outlining and scene work, then the creative writing coordinator managing drafting and voice consistency, quality integration for continuity and craft enforcement, and finally the beta-reading lenses that evaluate romance, fantasy, craft, sensitivity, and originality aspects.\\n\\nI\'m also tracking three cross-cutting specialists that report directly to the orchestrator: continuity tracking, series knowledge management, and craft tracking. Now I need to map out the routing logic across the pipeline phases—concept through worldbuilding, character, plotting, style, drafting, revision, beta-reading, and polish—keeping in mind the configuration limits like maxGapCycles and maxWriterReviewerRetries. The orchestrator will need to handle sequential phase routing with proper ordering.\\n\\nFor the coordinators, I\'m looking at how each one manages its internal flow with auditor feedback loops. The depth-2 coordinators like concept and style will route through their specialists and loop back if audits fail, while revision coordinates a chain of editors before finalizing chapters. I need to map where the cross-cutting agents inject themselves—continuity and craft tracking during drafting, and series knowledge management after polish wraps.\\n\\nBuilding the routing tables now... I\'m setting the retry bounds with maxWriterReviewerRetries at 10 for revision cycles and maxAuditorRetries at 3, then structuring the routing.json file to organize orchestrators, coordinators, and sub-coordinators with their routing tables, dispatch orders, and loop configurations.\\n\\nNow I\'m mapping out the auditor loop pattern: dispatch children sequentially, run the auditor, then either mark complete on pass, re-dispatch failed children up to the retry limit on failure, or escalate to blocked status. Let me work through each coordinator\'s routing logic, starting with the Concept Coordinator and its children—concept-developer, craft-profile-selector, and concept-auditor—defining how status checks and dispatches flow between them.\\n\\nContinuing with the Worldbuilding Coordinator, which orchestrates physical-world-coordinator and systems-world-coordinator before running the worldbuilding-auditor, following the same dispatch-and-audit pattern.\\n\\nNow I\'m mapping out the Character Coordinator\'s flow, which chains core-characters-coordinator through ensemble-coordinator before the character-auditor validates everything, with blocked states propagating up at each stage.\\n\\nThe Plotting and Style Coordinators follow the same cascading pattern—each has its own auditor that either completes the chain or triggers a retry from the beginning if it fails within the retry limit.\\n\\nNow I\'m mapping out the Drafting Coordinator, which operates differently since it loops through chapters. Each chapter goes through creative writing, then quality integration, then auditing—and if the auditor passes, we move to the next chapter or finish entirely.\\n\\nFor the Revision Coordinator, I\'m seeing a similar per-chapter pattern but with three sequential editing passes: developmental, line, and copy editing, followed by a chapter reviser and revision auditor. If the auditor fails within retry limits, it loops back.\\n\\nThe Beta-Reading Coordinator runs two lens coordinators in parallel for each chapter—genre and craft perspectives—then synthesizes their feedback and audits the results, with failed audits triggering a re-dispatch of both lenses.\\n\\nNext comes the Polish Coordinator, which handles polishing and summary generation per chapter sequentially, then assembles the final delivery once all chapters are complete.\\n\\nBelow that are four specialized world-building coordinators: one chains geography through culture to history, another sequences magic systems into political structures, a third develops the protagonist and romance arc together, and the last handles the ensemble cast.\\n\\nThen I\'m mapping out the structural design coordinator that sequences story structure selection into dual-arc building into tension mapping, followed by chapter design that chains outlining into scene-beat design, and creative writing that handles drafting and voice maintenance per chapter.\\n\\nQuality integration runs continuity and craft enforcement sequentially per chapter, while the genre and craft lenses run their beta readers in parallel to catch romance, fantasy, craft, sensitivity, and originality issues.\\n\\nNow I\'m building out the complete routing.json configuration... I need to clarify the distinction here—the orchestrator I\'m designing routes through the creative phases specific to the romantic-fantasy-writer system, not the factory\'s generic passes. So the phase sequence is concept through polish, with cross-cutting concerns like the continuity tracker and craft tracker invoked at specific points in the drafting and revision cycle.\\n\\nLooking at what each cross-cutting agent actually needs as input, I can see the orchestrator has to dispatch them strategically: the continuity tracker pulls from chapter drafts and world/character/plot data during drafting and revision, the craft tracker monitors drafts against the craft profile and plot structure, and the series knowledge manager activates after polish or during concept work for sequels. So the routing logic needs to know exactly when to invoke each one based on the phase and what data\'s available. I\'m realizing the orchestrator should dispatch these cross-cutting services at specific points in the workflow—craft-tracker gets initialized after plotting and then updated during drafting, continuity-tracker is handled directly by the chapter-drafter as it writes, and series-kb-manager runs after polish completes. promote to series KB\\n\\nWait, but they are listed as separate agents. Let me think about this differently. The orchestrator dispatches them as cross-cutting utility passes:\\n\\n1. concept → 2. worldbuilding → 3. character → 4. plotting → 5. style → 6. drafting (which internally handles per-chapter) → 7. revision → 8. beta-reading → 9. polish → 10. series-kb-manager → 11. craft-tracker (finalize) → done\\n\\nActually the simplest pattern is to treat craft-tracker and continuity-tracker as on-demand utility agents that the orchestrator dispatches at specific transition points. Let me just make them part of the main flow.\\n\\nSo the orchestrator routes through each phase sequentially—concept through polish—with craft-tracker initializing after style and continuity-tracker running final verification before promoting everything to the series knowledge base. The beta-reading-auditor gates quality, so if critical or major issues surface, those chapters loop back through revision before re-entering beta-reading—this creates a bounded revision cycle at the orchestrator level. Now '... 101302 more characters
