egends and myths characters might reference (these feed foreshadowing and thematic resonance)',\\n            '6. Document historical events that created current conflicts (wars, political shifts, magical upheavals)',\\n            '7. Establish how history shapes character motivations: what past traumas or victories drive protagonists and antagonists?',\\n            '8. Ensure timeline is internally consistent per INV-002 — no contradictory historical claims',\\n            '9. Write world-bible/history.json with eras (ID: ERA-NNN), historical figures, legends, and timeline clarity',\\n            '10. Include upstream references to geography.json and culture.json (per INV-029)'\\n        ],\\n        'invariants': ['INV-002', 'INV-029'],\\n        'artifact_reads': ['story-concept.json', 'world-bible/geography.json', 'world-bible/culture.json'],\\n        'artifact_writes': ['world-bible/history.json'],\\n        'result_codes': ['completed', 'blocked'],\\n    },\\n\\n    'romantic-fantasy-writer-magic-system-designer': {\\n        'role': 'Design a comprehensive magic system following Sanderson\\\\'s Laws (INV-048/T10): rules, costs, limitations, known practitioners, power levels. Hard magic requires clear rules before conflict resolution.',\\n        'process_steps': [\\n            '1. Read story-concept.json — assess how magic serves the plot and romance',\\n            '2. Decide hardness level: hard (clear rules), soft (mysterious), or hybrid',\\n            '3. Define the core magic rules: what magic can and cannot do, fundamental mechanics',\\n            '4. For hard magic: document cost (energy, sacrifice, time) per spell or power level',\\n            '5. For hard magic: document limitations — what magic explicitly cannot do (prevents deus ex machina per INV-009)',\\n            '6. Establish power levels: practitioners from novice to master, what each level can achieve',\\n            '7. Identify known practitioners in the story world and their power